# Milestone 2: Integrate the AI model

Once the scanner works, we will add the first real AI feature.

## Which model will we use?

[Converting YAMNet audio detection model for TensorFlow Lite | Medium](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/i-_KWhUfydhpwuk8b1J4yQXvHWa_03VOY4AlsNvpH9EPJJd9YbN3B0JXG3qYgo5fX43vtic6TjM63eND4QmeH-LYImdxkbOypKoSCb1gdSS51RASFdynae7sTwRuNoX4XAk6M2akl7iZwISRb68FGKxookWdfi064qH7qgsVuxg?purpose=inline)

YAMNet — pretrained environmental sound classifier

YAMNet recognizes 521 audio event classes. It expects a mono waveform sampled at 16 kHz, with 15,600 float32 samples per input window.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.tensorflow.org\&sz=32)

TensorFlow+1



This allows us to build a baseline without training an AI model from scratch.

[Read the official YAMNet tutorial](https://www.tensorflow.org/hub/tutorials/yamnet?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### Important implementation detail

Our microphone code already requests 16 kHz PCM audio, but that alone does not make it ready for YAMNet. We must:

1. Convert PCM16 samples into normalized float32 values between -1 and 1.
2. Accumulate samples into windows of exactly 15,600 samples.
3. Load a compatible `.tflite` model and its matching label list.
4. Run inference using the `tflite_flutter` interpreter.
5. Display the top sound predictions and confidence scores.
6. Handle model initialization and inference errors without freezing the UI.

The Flutter package is documented here: [tflite_flutter on pub.dev](https://pub.dev/packages/tflite_flutter?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Its current documentation notes an Android API level requirement of 26 for its dynamic-library setup, so we will verify the Android minimum SDK before integrating it.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://pub.dev\&sz=32)

Flutter package



We will implement this in a separate `lib/services/sound_classifier.dart` file rather than putting AI code inside `main.dart`.

